# L §3.4 (round 7c): "`cpi` is an input or a def keyed by one time index (a level built from an inflation row is a
# def)".  Reading rent (2 000.00 PLN in 2026 prices) at law date d gives `num PLN` = value x cpi[d] / cpi[2026]: in
# 2026, 2 000 PLN; in 2027, with cpi[2027] = cpi[2026] x (1 + 5 %), 2 000 x 1.05 = 2 100 PLN.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-10-18
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2028-12-31

source s "synthetic"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01

index ty = year 2026..2028

input inflation: num
input rent: money PLN prices 2026 by cpi

def cpi[y in ty]: num at start_of(y)
  rule initial = 1 when pos(y) == 1
  rule next = cpi[y - 1] * (1 + inflation) when pos(y) > 1

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  inflation = 5 %
  rent = 2 000.00 PLN

ask q1: rent law 2026-06-30
ask q2: rent law 2027-06-30
=== expect
q1: 2 000 PLN
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-10-18
q2: 2 100 PLN
  law date 2027-06-30, cut 2026-10-18
